+# BC250 v7: chunked TASK dispatches above the 4096 bound
+
+v6 capped every application task dispatch at 4096 total workgroups and rejected
+larger ones with VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT. v7 removes that wall for direct
+draws while keeping scratch memory bounded.
+
+## How it works
+
+- A direct vkCmdDrawMeshTasks(x,y,z) whose total exceeds BC250_TASK_CHUNK (4096)
+  is split by the driver into sequential sub-boxes of at most 4096 workgroups,
+  each emitted as [producer compute dispatch -> mesh indirect draw] before the
+  next chunk reuses the shared record/payload scratch. Queue ordering plus the
+  existing partial-flush discipline keeps the scratch coherent between chunks.
+- The application task shader still sees its original full-dispatch builtins: a
+  per-chunk slot table in scratch carries the sub-box origin and the original
+  (x,y,z), and the producer lowers gl_WorkGroupID/gl_NumWorkGroups to those
+  values instead of the hardware chunk values. Record/payload indexing stays
+  chunk-local, matching the mesh consumer's per-draw record index.
+- MESH gl_DrawID is emulated as continuous across chunks via a chunk_base push
+  constant (zero for single-chunk draws, so behavior there is unchanged).
+- Indirect records remain clamped to one chunk by the setup shader; indirect
+  draw_count remains capped at 4096. Larger workloads must use direct draws.
+
+## Advertised limits
+
+maxTaskWorkGroupTotalCount = 4194304 (2^22, the spec minimum), per-dimension
+65535. Dispatches above the total cap are still rejected at vkEndCommandBuffer.
+
+## Validated on this board (2026-09-11)
+
+- run-hybrid.sh: full suite PASS. task-limit now runs a 4097-group direct draw
+  and verifies payload integrity across the chunk boundary; task-draw-limit is
+  still rejected as before.
+- run-expand.sh, run-native-compact.sh: PASS (unchanged behavior).
+- dEQP-VK.mesh_shader.ext.misc.per_prim_block_output: Pass
+- dEQP-VK.mesh_shader.ext.misc.custom_attributes: Pass
+- dEQP-VK.mesh_shader.ext.misc.custom_attributes_and_task_shader: Pass
+- dEQP-VK.mesh_shader.ext.synchronization.other.barrier_across_secondary
+  (16384 task groups, secondary command buffers): Pass. Previously rejected.
+- dEQP-VK.mesh_shader.ext.misc.many_task_work_groups_x
+  (65535 x 8 x 8 = 4194240 task groups, ~1024 chunks): Pass in ~3s.
+  Previously rejected.
+- dEQP-VK.mesh_shader.ext.smoke.monolithic.mesh_task_shader_triangle: Pass
+- dEQP-VK.mesh_shader.ext.misc.many_task_mesh_work_groups_x (32768 mesh groups): Pass
+
+No GPU hang observed during any of the above. The BC250_USAGE diagnostic marker
+still prints "v5-diag"; identify the driver by its loaded library path/hash.
diff --git a/tests/bc250-native-mesh/README.md b/tests/bc250-native-mesh/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2af61d5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/bc250-native-mesh/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,172 @@
+# BC250 native mesh and hybrid TASK — third tested revision
+
+This patch series runs native MESH plus a COMPUTE-backed TASK producer on an
+AMD BC-250. It targets Mesa 26.2.1, upstream commit
+`da14d65e4499e66468094be52bff9ea0915a695e`.
+
+This is an experimental, nonconformant implementation. The resource and indirect
+restrictions from the first prototype have been substantially reduced, but this
+is not a game-ready driver release or a completed Vulkan conformance claim.
+
+## Revision 3 findings and limitations
+
+The supported TASK regression suite passes, including new zero-output MESH
+workgroups with 32 and 128 invocations and ordinary vertex rendering afterward.
+The driver now uses Mesa's existing GFX10 fully-culled-workgroup workaround in
+all three mesh allocation paths. A workgroup that produces no primitives emits
+a hardware-culled dummy primitive instead of a zero allocation.
+
+**Per-primitive parameter exports remain unsupported.** A minimal two-group test
+with distinct per-primitive colors rendered only one triangle and subsequently
+broke ordinary rendering. The native-only CTS comparison failed as well, so this
+is not confined to the hybrid TASK producer. Revision 3 rejects mesh shaders with
+live per-primitive parameter exports at binding: command-buffer finalization
+returns `VK_ERROR_FEATURE_NOT_PRESENT` before GPU submission. This check also
+covers cached shaders and shader-object binding. Per-vertex attributes remain
+available. The included `perprimitive.mesh` and `perprimitive.frag` preserve the
+reproducer, and the runner verifies rejection and subsequent normal rendering.
+
+The original public project's claim of validated per-primitive inputs must not
+be treated as general support on this development driver. Do not use earlier
+packages to bypass the new guard for normal workloads.
+
+Independent Vulkan CTS was built from official commit
+`659bbe6987197b4ff7ac20011261b92009286100`. This driver does **not** pass the full
+mesh-shader smoke group or Vulkan conformance. See `cts-v3-original-smoke.log`
+for the final results: 9 passed, 2 failed at the per-primitive export guard,
+and 2 reported unsupported fragment-shading-rate features.
+
+One CTS case, `partial_usage_without_compaction`, writes unused output vertices
+before `SetMeshOutputsEXT`. This violates the mesh shader instruction ordering
+rule in the [SPIR-V extension specification](https://github.khronos.org/SPIRV-Registry/extensions/EXT/SPV_EXT_mesh_shader.html).
+A separately modified diagnostic moved the count call before those writes; it
+stopped corrupting later rendering but still failed image comparison. A compact
+variant using per-vertex colors passed. Modified diagnostic results are not
+counted as official CTS passes. The CTS source was restored before the final run.
+
+The unsuccessful global compute-ABI and fragment-count experiments were reverted.
+The system Mesa installation remains unchanged; this package is an opt-in test
+build, not a production driver.
+
+## Verified on the BC250
+
+The headless 64x64 readback suite verifies actual pixels and storage-buffer values:
+
+- Native MESH direct, indirect, indirect-count and multi-record draws.
+- TASK payloads, distinct task groups in X/Y/Z, zero-output tasks and mesh fan-out.
+- Payload atomics/barriers across 64 TASK invocations and command-buffer replay.
+- Application push constants, including offsets 240–255 used across TASK, MESH
+  and fragment stages, and changes between draws.
+- Traditional descriptor sets: UBO, SSBO, two descriptor sets, and a dynamic UBO
+  offset. The same buffers are consumed by TASK and MESH.
+- Restoration of an application compute pipeline and its different dynamic UBO
+  offset after hybrid draws, verified through compute-written buffer values.
+- Indirect TASK and indirect-count TASK commands; application DrawID is preserved
+  in both TASK and MESH across multiple input records.
+- Zero-sized indirect work and zero draw counts. A previously submitted command
+  buffer can be replayed with its indirect count changed from two to zero,
+  producing a blank frame without stale work.
+- GPU-generated indirect records and count: a compute shader produces commands,
+  a Vulkan barrier makes them available, and TASK→MESH renders them using live
+  descriptors and push constants. No driver-side CPU readback is used.
+
+The original triangle produces 968 red pixels. Two separate triangles produce
+495 red and 495 green pixels. The compute restoration test reads the expected
+`deadbeef` uniform sentinel and the current application push-constant values.
+Native wave64 direct rendering was also verified during initial bring-up.
+
+## How it works
+
+The native baseline keeps the board's real GFX1013 identity. Its essential
+hardware fix excludes NGG MESH fast launch from the ordinary GFX10 vertex-group
+clamp. GE_CNTL.VERT_GRP_SIZE must equal ES_VERTS_PER_SUBGRP for this path; applying
+the normal clamp produced zero and locked this board. AMD PAL's fast-launch
+programming provides the matching rule. The GFX10 startup barrier remains.
+
+TASK is cloned and lowered to a private COMPUTE pipeline on the graphics queue.
+It writes one native mesh dispatch record and one payload record per TASK
+workgroup. Completion/cache operations precede native indirect MESH execution.
+An internal DrawID chooses the payload; the application DrawID is separate.
+
+Application push constants are copied intact into per-draw GPU storage. Their
+shader loads become global reads through a private pointer. The private constant
+block is 56 bytes; application values retain their normal 256-byte address space.
+Graphics descriptors are temporarily supplied to the producer and the previous
+application compute descriptor state is restored afterward. TASK dynamic
+visibility is mapped to the internal compute layout.
+
+For indirect calls, a setup compute shader reads the input dimensions and optional
+count, bounds-checks them, and writes a compute dispatch plus the corresponding
+native mesh record count. Input records above the GPU-supplied count are skipped
+before their argument data is read. Scratch is reused across records only after
+previous consumers complete; memory use is bounded by task capacity and payload
+stride instead of multiplying by the number of indirect records.
+
+No native TASK/ACE ring or generation spoof is used. TASK-related barrier scopes
+include the graphics queue's compute engine. No system driver is overwritten.
+
+## Remaining limitations
+
+`RADV_BC250_HYBRID_TASK=1` enables this development path. Without it, native mesh
+remains available and TASK stays unadvertised. The opt-in hides graphics pipeline
+libraries, shader objects, descriptor buffers/heaps, pipeline binaries and device-
+generated commands so applications can select supported fallback paths. These
+extensions remain available when the opt-in is disabled.
+
+- Maximum 4096 TASK workgroups per draw, per-dimension maximum 4096, payload 16 KiB.
+  The total TASK limit is below the Vulkan-required minimum. Direct out-of-range
+  commands report an error; indirect dimensions outside this development limit
+  become zero work in the setup shader.
+- Maximum 4096 indirect input records per call under the opt-in. The scheduler
+  emits separate setup/producer/consumer sequences and prioritizes correctness;
+  performance and larger dispatch scheduling remain work to do.
+- Descriptor-buffer/descriptor-heap modes, TASK shader objects, TASK graphics
+  pipeline libraries, multiview, indirect-bindable TASK pipelines, TASK binary
+  capture and graphics binary imports under the opt-in are unsupported.
+- Traditional buffer descriptor paths are tested. Textures, images, bindless
+  indexing and every descriptor combination are not yet validated.
+- CullPrimitive remains quarantined. The original project reports hard locks
+  on this path; no speculative primitive-export bit change has been made.
+- Large mesh-output scratch, secondary/simultaneously reused command buffers,
+  conditional-rendering edge cases, query/statistics behavior, per-stage
+  robustness overrides and full Vulkan CTS have not been validated.
+- Graphics shader caching for hybrid TASK pipelines is bypassed because the
+  private producer is not represented in the normal graphics cache entry.
+
+## Apply, build and reproduce
+
+On the exact Mesa base above, apply in order:
+
+```sh
+git apply bc250-native-baseline.patch
+git apply bc250-hybrid-task-v3.patch
+meson setup build --buildtype=debugoptimized -Db_ndebug=false \
+  -Dvulkan-drivers=amd -Dgallium-drivers= -Dplatforms=x11,wayland \
+  -Dllvm=disabled -Dvideo-codecs= -Dglx=disabled -Degl=disabled \
+  -Dgles1=disabled -Dgles2=disabled -Dopengl=false -Dbuild-tests=false
+ninja -C build -j4
+bash tests/bc250-native-mesh/run-hybrid.sh
+```
+
+The second patch includes the initial hybrid prototype plus resource and indirect
+work; do not also apply the old `bc250-hybrid-task.patch` on top of it.
+
+The runner requires a C compiler, glslangValidator, Python3 and libvulkan.so.1.
+It creates a local ICD manifest selecting the build library and writes logs/PPM
+readbacks under `tests/bc250-native-mesh/build-results`. `BC250_MESA_ROOT` and
+`BC250_TEST_RESULTS` can override those locations. Nothing is installed globally.
+
+Tests ran on CachyOS with kernel 7.1.8-1.44-cachyos-bore-bc250. Suspend/resume is
+broken on this machine; its sleep targets were masked for the current boot.
+Those runtime masks reset after reboot. A test timeout cannot recover a GPU hang.
+
+## Source context
+
+Native enablement was ported from lonewolf0622's public patch. The inspected
+public revision d6ba686 contained a TASK design but no usable implementation.
+The hybrid code and tests here are new experimental work following that design.
+
+Project: https://github.com/lonewolf0622/BC250-Native-Mesh-Shaders-
+Hardware programming reference: GPUOpen-Drivers/pal,
+`src/core/hw/gfxip/gfx9/gfx9UniversalCmdBuffer.cpp`, CalcGeCntl, commit
+`c5e800072a32f68b6ccc4422936d96167c6e0728`.
